Actor-critic reinforcement learning is a popular approach due to its efficiency in policy optimization. However, policy representation and its gradient estimation pose significant challenges, particularly in high-dimensional action spaces. To address these limitations, we propose a novel method that integrates Hamiltonian Monte Carlo to generate samples from the posterior distribution of the policy, leveraging these samples to estimate the policy gradient. We also present theoretical convergence analysis for this actor-critic framework and demonstrate its effectiveness through high-dimensional experiments. This technique has the added benefit of reducing action-space dimensionality. Our implementation is a straightforward modification of existing actor-critic models, making it easily adaptable for real-world applications. Additionally, the source code is publicly accessible at the specified repository. 

